  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "A systematic review synthesized 87 peer-reviewed studies published between 2000 and March 2025 to evaluate global e-bike adoption across individual, behavioral, environmental, and policy dimensions. The analysis shows that while middle-aged, older, and mobility-constrained adults adopt e-bikes primarily for health and active travel, general populations increasingly use them for commuting and gig-economy delivery, with usage patterns strongly dictated by local land use and transit availability.",
      "why": "By structuring scattered international literature into a unified framework, the review demonstrates that e-bike adoption is driven by contextual infrastructure rather than uniform user preferences, challenging generic active-transport promotion policies in favor of spatial and equitable governance."
    }
  },
